{{- /* chezmoi:modify-template */ -}}
{{- /*
  Selective field control for ~/.claude/settings.json.

  Why: Claude Code's /config slash command writes user-toggled settings
  (alwaysThinkingEnabled, useAutoModeDuringPlan, voiceEnabled, etc.) directly
  into ~/.claude/settings.json, which would create drift against any plain
  chezmoi template. There is no user-level ~/.claude/settings.local.json that
  Claude Code reads, so a clean split into local/shared isn't possible at the
  user scope.

  How: this is a chezmoi modify-template (https://www.chezmoi.io/user-guide/
  manage-different-types-of-file/#modify-templates). It receives the current
  ~/.claude/settings.json on .chezmoi.stdin, overlays the stable fields below
  via setValueAtPath, and writes the merged result back.

  Three categories of field, not two:

    STABLE       overwritten from here on every apply, whatever the live file
                 says: permissions, hooks, statusLine, skillOverrides,
                 cleanupPeriodDays, autoUpdatesChannel, remoteControlAtStartup,
                 effortLevel, and the seven extraKnownMarketplaces entries this
                 repo declares.
    FREE-DRIFT   passed through untouched: alwaysThinkingEnabled, voiceEnabled,
                 useAutoModeDuringPlan and anything else /config adds.
    enabledPlugins  the ROSTER is fixed here and the per-plugin STATE is not.
                 Membership of the declared list is stable; a DECLARED plugin
                 the live file disables (JSON boolean false) or pins (JSON array
                 of version constraints) keeps that across applies. An
                 UNDECLARED one keeps nothing, the write is whole-value, so its
                 entry is erased. The price is that this repo no longer
                 re-asserts plugin state at all, and that a live disable is
                 invisible to `chezmoi status`, `chezmoi diff` and `just d`.
                 See the plugin block below.

  Promote a /config toggle to STABLE by adding it to one of the setValueAtPath
  calls below.
*/ -}}
{{- /* .chezmoi.stdin is only set when chezmoi runs the modify template
       with a real target file. Use safe access so the template still
       renders cleanly under `chezmoi execute-template` for ad-hoc testing.

       TRIMMED before the emptiness test, and that is a correctness
       requirement rather than tidiness. An empty string is falsy and a string
       of whitespace is not, so an untrimmed read sends a blank file to
       fromJson, which HARD ERRORS on it (`error calling fromJson: EOF`,
       measured 2026-08-02 on chezmoi 2.62.3 and 2.71.1). A modify-template
       that errors aborts the WHOLE apply, not just this target: every later
       target and every run_after_ script is skipped, and the corrupt file is
       left in place, so permissions.deny is not restored either. A file
       holding one newline is what an editor leaves after saving an emptied
       buffer, so this is the cheapest shape to produce.

       KNOWN LIMIT, and it is repaired OUTSIDE this template. Trimming only
       fixes the shapes that are EMPTY once trimmed. A live file that is
       non-empty and does not parse (a write truncated mid-object, a hand edit
       with a stray character) aborts the apply from here, and no template can
       catch it: chezmoi's three JSON readers (fromJson, fromJsonc, fromYaml)
       all fail the template on bad input, and Go's text/template has no
       recover, so there is nothing to fall back FROM (measured 2026-08-02, and
       test/unit/claude-enabled-plugins.sh pins the boundary with a case per
       shape). So the repair runs BEFORE this template instead:
       .chezmoiscripts/run_before_12-quarantine-unparseable-claude-settings.sh
       moves an unreadable settings file into ~/workspaces/backups and leaves
       `{}` in its place, which this template then rebuilds from.

       The move is not free, and the script says so at the time. Plugin state is
       the one managed thing this template cannot rebuild: it is READ from the
       live file rather than declared here, so with nothing to read every
       declared plugin renders `true`, which re-enables every disabled plugin
       and drops every version pin. Nothing reports that afterwards either (see
       the plugin block below), so restore it with `claude plugin disable <id>`
       per plugin, reading the quarantined copy for the set that was disabled.
       Everything else this template manages does come back from here on the
       next apply, and free-drift keys are lost as before. */ -}}
{{- $stdin := index .chezmoi "stdin" | default "" | trim -}}
{{- $merged := dict -}}
{{- if $stdin -}}
{{-   $merged = fromJson $stdin -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- /* The live file's own enabledPlugins, read HERE so no setValueAtPath call
       below can overwrite it before the plugin block consumes it.

       Both containers are kind-guarded to a MAP before they are indexed, and
       that is a correctness requirement rather than caution. Measured
       2026-08-02 on chezmoi 2.62.3 and 2.71.1, which agreed on every idiom:
       `index` on an untyped nil is a HARD error, and `hasKey` is nil-safe but
       is a HARD error on a non-map (`wrong type for value; expected
       map[string]interface {}`). An unguarded read kills the WHOLE render, and
       chezmoi then writes no settings.json at all, taking permissions.deny,
       every hook, statusLine and every skillOverrides entry with it. Both
       containers legitimately arrive as something other than a map: the live
       file is absent or empty on a fresh machine, and enabledPlugins is JSON
       null once anything writes the key without entries. A nil check alone is
       not enough, which is why the guard tests for `map` rather than for
       presence. */ -}}
{{- $livePlugins := dict -}}
{{- if eq (kindOf $merged) "map" -}}
{{-   $liveEnabledPlugins := index $merged "enabledPlugins" -}}
{{-   if eq (kindOf $liveEnabledPlugins) "map" -}}
{{-     $livePlugins = $liveEnabledPlugins -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- /* --- stable: scalars and policy --- */ -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "cleanupPeriodDays" 365 $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "autoUpdatesChannel" "stable" $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "remoteControlAtStartup" true $merged -}}
{{- /* Spinner tips off everywhere (operator, 2026-08-11). */ -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "spinnerTipsEnabled" false $merged -}}
{{- /* xhigh is the maximum effortLevel enum value (low/medium/high/xhigh) */ -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "effortLevel" "xhigh" $merged -}}
{{- /* Stable env passthroughs (operator, 2026-08-05). Per-key on purpose so any
      env var the operator sets by hand drifts freely, the same per-key pattern
      the skill-override block above uses. */ -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "env.CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS" "1" $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "env.CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_FEEDBACK_SURVEY" "1" $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "env.DISABLE_TELEMETRY" "1" $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "env.DISABLE_ERROR_REPORTING" "1" $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "env.DISABLE_NON_ESSENTIAL_MODEL_CALLS" "1" $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "env.CLAUDE_AUTOCOMPACT_PCT_OVERRIDE" "75" $merged -}}
{{- /* Empty attribution strings turn off the Claude co-author/footer natively,
      which is what the global no-AI-trailer rule has enforced by hand. */ -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "attribution.commit" "" $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "attribution.pr" "" $merged -}}

{{- /* PATH SEMANTICS ARE LOAD-BEARING IN THE DENY LIST. A bare or ./-prefixed
       pattern is CURRENT-DIRECTORY relative, so `Read(.ssh/id_*)` in USER
       settings matched a project's own .ssh directory and never ~/.ssh, which
       is the file it was written to protect (Claude Code permissions docs;
       found 2026-08-05 by a security review, after CLAUDE.md had claimed that
       protection for months). Home-anchored rules need the ~/ prefix. The
       first four entries stay relative ON PURPOSE: a project's own .env,
       secrets/ and credentials.json are exactly what those mean.

       KNOWN LIMIT, unchanged by this fix: deny only covers the Bash commands
       Claude Code recognizes as file reads, and the allow list carries
       `Bash(grep *)` and `Bash(find *)`, either of which reads a key anyway.
       Moot at defaultMode bypassPermissions; it matters the moment the mode
       tightens. */ -}}
{{- $permissions := dict
    "allow" (list
      "Read"
      "Grep"
      "Glob"
      "WebFetch"
      "WebSearch"
      "Bash(find *)"
      "Bash(cat *)"
      "Bash(ls *)"
      "Bash(head *)"
      "Bash(tail *)"
      "Bash(wc *)"
      "Bash(grep *)"
      "Bash(tree *)")
    "deny" (list
      "Read(.env)"
      "Read(.env.*)"
      "Read(secrets/**)"
      "Read(credentials.json)"
      "Read(~/.aws/credentials)"
      "Read(~/.ssh/id_*)"
      "Read(~/.ssh/*_rsa)"
      "Read(~/.ssh/*_ed25519)"
      "Read(~/.claude/.credentials.json)"
      "Read(~/.codex/auth.json)"
      "Read(~/.config/pns/config.toml)"
      "Read(~/.config/osquery/webhook-secret)"
      "Read(~/.hermes/.env)"
      "Read(~/**/*.kdbx)")
    "defaultMode" "bypassPermissions" -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "permissions" $permissions $merged -}}

{{- /* --- stable: hooks (paths use $home for portability) ---
     SessionStart is herdr's agent-state integration: `herdr integration
     install claude` writes exactly this entry (and owns the hook FILE at
     ~/.claude/hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh, which is deliberately unmanaged).
     Declared here so an apply keeps it; on a fresh machine the entry points at
     a file the first `herdr integration install claude` creates.

     The blocked notification hangs off PermissionRequest, NOT off
     Notification/permission_prompt, and it is deliberately NOT async. Both
     halves are load bearing. PermissionRequest runs BEFORE the prompt is
     shown and the harness awaits it, so it is the only seam through which the
     hook can hand the payload to moshi-hook while the prompt is still worth
     raising a card about; the operator's answer then comes back through
     moshi's own bridge typing into that prompt, NOT through this hook's exit
     code (measured 2026-08-29: approve and deny both leave moshi-hook exiting
     0 with empty stdout). NOT async is the second half: the harness waits
     for a non-async hook, so the card is registered before the prompt is
     drawn instead of racing it. What an async hook would do on this event
     is unverified, so that spelling is a deliberate choice and not a
     measured one. And running the hook on BOTH events would notify twice
     for one prompt (PermissionRequest fires, then the prompt appears and
     Notification fires), so this is one seam per harness, matching Codex's
     PermissionRequest entry in
     hooks/codex/install-hooks.sh. Notification carries a QUOTA-ONLY entry:
     alerter held the slot for a local sound and was the last notification
     path on this machine that bypassed the presence engine, and for TOOL
     approvals the PermissionRequest hook above and the unanswered-approval
     nag already raise what it raised, so it was deleted rather than kept
     for a second opinion (5d30d362) and the slot sat empty until this one.
     Sandbox NETWORK approvals are covered by neither: Claude Code's
     PermissionRequest hooks do not run for them, so deleting alerter left
     that class with no repository-managed alert and no nag. That gap is
     known and filed (backlog B39), not fixed here. alerter itself stays
     installed: the osquery pipeline is its real caller.

     THE ONE MATCHER IS AN EXACT PIPE-SEPARATED ALLOWLIST OF THREE QUOTA
     AUTO-RESUME TYPES, never a wildcard: a wildcard entry here would
     double-fire against the canonical lifecycle hooks for the reason stated
     just above (a permission prompt against PermissionRequest, an
     elicitation against the Elicitation hook, and so on). `agent_needs_input`
     and `agent_completed` are deliberately left off the allowlist rather
     than wired: the former may duplicate an ordinary asked or blocked event
     and the latter combines success and failure in one notification type, so
     either needs a live capture before it can be mapped honestly. Async,
     because Claude Code hooks block by default and the quota arm dispatches
     through the full delivery plan before it ever reaches the attempt guard,
     so a synchronous hook would delay Claude Code's own quota transition on
     every wait. Routed to ONE handler (`pns hook quota`) rather than three
     declarations, which is one seam to keep in sync instead of three that can
     drift apart.

     ONE Stop entry: the engine measures the turn and reports it in the same
     pass, where a separate pulse hook used to decide the tier again on its
     own.

     ConfigChange fires when Claude Code's own configuration changes underneath
     a session (its own reference documents five sources: `user_settings`,
     `project_settings`, `local_settings`, `policy_settings`, `skills`, and a
     `file_path` when the harness names one). Routed as an OBSERVATION, like
     the quota arm beside it: it is a configuration audit trail, not a turn
     needing the operator's attention, so it must not clear a wait, renew a
     lease or claim the return moment, and it makes NO promise of
     completeness (server-managed settings, macOS managed preferences and
     Windows registry policy are all outside what this event can see). ONE
     CARD PER RECEIVED EVENT rather than once ever: several live sessions, a
     changed skill, or a corrupt-file recovery's own intermediate write can
     each raise their own event, and this fires again for each one rather
     than coalescing them. THE ONE MATCHER IS AN EXACT PIPE-SEPARATED
     ALLOWLIST OF THE FIVE DOCUMENTED SOURCES, in the quota matcher's own
     style, and the binary keeps its OWN copy of the same five-value
     allowlist rather than trusting this matcher alone (`config_source_label`
     in main.rs), since the payload parser accepts any string. Async, for the
     reference's own strongest reason: a SYNCHRONOUS ConfigChange hook can
     otherwise BLOCK the configuration change from taking effect
     (`policy_settings` excepted) by exiting 2 or returning a structured
     `decision`; an ordinary non-zero exit does not block it (a crash exiting
     101 changes nothing about whether the setting lands), and async drops
     the whole exit-code decision path regardless, which is what actually
     keeps pns from ever holding up or refusing a configuration change it
     exists only to audit. No `/dev/null`
     redirect is needed here the way PostModelSwitch's command carries one:
     the reference's own exception list for stdout reaching the model's
     context names `UserPromptSubmit`, `UserPromptExpansion`, `SessionStart`
     and `PostModelSwitch`, and ConfigChange is not among them.

     PostModelSwitch fires whenever the session's model changes; it is routed
     as an OBSERVATION, never a first delivery, so it clears no wait, renews
     no lease and claims no return moment (only a session's own First event
     does that). Restricted to `source == "auto"`: `command`, `picker` and
     `sdk` are a deliberate choice, not news, and `resume` is a state-only
     audit record left for a follow-up. BOTH halves of the declaration are
     load-bearing, per the hooks reference: PostModelSwitch is one of the
     events whose exit-0 stdout is delivered to the model on the next turn,
     and that is true whether or not the hook is async, so `async true` alone
     does not keep the engine's own `pns: ` print out of the model's context;
     the command redirects stdout to `/dev/null` itself, which a command hook
     with no `args` runs under `sh -c`, so the redirect is honoured.

     StopFailure fires INSTEAD of Stop when a turn dies on an API error, so
     without this entry a dead pane gets no card at all and the dead turn's
     start marker is left for the next turn to inherit. Async like Stop, since
     it is a report and nobody needs its exit code, and deliberately NO
     matcher: the harness matches a StopFailure matcher against the error
     TEXT, and every dead turn is news.

     PermissionDenied fires when the auto-mode classifier refuses a tool call
     on its own, which the operator otherwise learns only by reading the pane.
     Async for a STRONGER reason than Stop's: the harness awaits this hook's
     answer to learn whether the model may retry the refused call, and an async
     hook is backgrounded with its late answer discarded, so pns can never
     appear to answer a question it has no business answering. Deliberately NO
     matcher, and note that a matcher here would filter by TOOL NAME rather
     than by the reason, which is the opposite of StopFailure's error text.

     PostToolBatch fires when an assistant tool batch RESOLVES, and it is the
     nag's clearing signal: the record written when the operator was blocked on
     a permission prompt is removed here, and an answered marker is written in
     its place. It is the ONLY single declaration covering both operator verbs,
     because a DENIED tool call still produces a tool_result and so resolves the
     batch rather than skipping it, where PermissionDenied covers only the
     classifier's own refusals. Note honestly what the signal IS, and what it
     therefore does NOT promise: it is the BATCH RESOLVING, not the operator
     answering. So a tool approved in ten seconds whose run outlives the
     schedule is nudged about anyway, and an approval answered while another
     approval's card is being built can still be named on that card, because
     several waiting approvals coalesce into one. Both residuals are stated in
     the [nag] block of the pns config as well. What this entry does buy is that
     every approval is nudged at most once and that a batch which has resolved
     stops the nudge that has not gone out yet. Async, for the simplest reason on this list: it delivers nothing and
     nobody reads its answer, so the harness must not wait for it. Deliberately
     NO matcher, since every batch is a clearing signal. Stop and StopFailure
     clear the same record without any declaration at all, which is the backstop
     for a batch payload over pns's 1MB stdin cap, for an operator who escapes
     the prompt instead of answering it, and for the window between a merge and
     the apply that installs this entry.

     Elicitation fires when a connected MCP server asks the operator for input
     and holds its tool call open waiting. It rides the `asked` word: the
     interruption is the same one AskUserQuestion raises, and it is answered at
     the pane the harness is already holding open. Async, and for the strongest
     reason any entry here has: this hook runs BEFORE the dialog is shown and
     the harness reads a decision out of it, where exit code 2 alone declines
     the elicitation outright and the MCP server sees a refusal with no
     explanation. An async hook is backgrounded with an empty answer, so pns
     cannot answer a question it has no business answering. Deliberately NO
     matcher, and note that a matcher here filters by MCP SERVER NAME, which is
     neither PreToolUse's tool name nor StopFailure's error text.
     --- */ -}}
{{- $home := .chezmoi.homeDir -}}
{{- $hooks := dict
    "UserPromptSubmit" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook prompt" $home)))))
    "Stop" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook stop" $home)))))
    "PostModelSwitch" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook model-switch >/dev/null" $home)))))
    "StopFailure" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook stop-failure" $home)))))
    "Notification" (list (dict
      "matcher" "quota_auto_resume_fired|quota_auto_resume_stale|quota_auto_resume_disabled"
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook quota" $home)))))
    "ConfigChange" (list (dict
      "matcher" "user_settings|project_settings|local_settings|policy_settings|skills"
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook config-change" $home)))))
    "PermissionRequest" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook blocked" $home)))))
    "PermissionDenied" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook denied" $home)))))
    "Elicitation" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook asked" $home)))))
    "PostToolBatch" (list (dict
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "async" true
        "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook resolved" $home)))))
    "PostToolUse" (list
      (dict
        "matcher" "AskUserQuestion"
        "hooks" (list (dict
          "type" "command"
          "async" true
          "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook asked" $home))))
      (dict
        "matcher" "ExitPlanMode"
        "hooks" (list (dict
          "type" "command"
          "async" true
          "command" (printf "%s/.local/libexec/pns/pns hook plan-ready" $home)))))
    "SessionStart" (list (dict
      "matcher" "*"
      "hooks" (list (dict
        "type" "command"
        "command" (printf "bash '%s/.claude/hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh' session" $home)
        "timeout" 10)))) -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "hooks" $hooks $merged -}}

{{- /* --- stable: statusLine (path uses $home) --- */ -}}
{{- $statusLine := dict
    "type" "command"
    "command" (printf "bash %s/.claude/statusline-command.sh" $home) -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "statusLine" $statusLine $merged -}}

{{- /* --- the declared plugin ROSTER, ON by default, with the machine's own
       per-plugin STATE preserved. The roster is stable; the state is not.

       This block REPLACES enabledPlugins wholesale, it does not merge into it,
       because setValueAtPath writes the value at the path. So every entry the
       live file holds for an id missing from the list below is ERASED on the
       next apply, silently. Every MARKETPLACE plugin meant to stay on must be
       listed: Claude Code builds its marketplace-plugin load set out of these
       keys, so erasing an id stops that plugin loading (read out of the shipped
       2.1.220 binary on 2026-08-02). A plugin under ~/.claude/skills/ resolves
       the other way, its manifest default (ON) applies when no entry is
       present, so listing a skill is not what keeps it on and erasing a `false`
       for one REVOKES that disable. Those are not in this roster and are not
       meant to be. test/unit/claude-enabled-plugins.sh fails when this set and
       the roster disagree.

       The list is names only, not name-to-value pairs: membership means "this
       repo manages this plugin, and it is ON unless the machine already says
       something else". Claude Code 2.1.220's settings schema declares this key
       as `record(string, union([array(string), boolean, undefined]))`, and BOTH
       members of that union are the machine's to set, so both are carried
       through: a boolean (what `claude plugin disable <id>` writes, and the
       only supported way to stop a plugin's code running) and an array (what
       that schema calls the extended format with version constraints, a plugin
       held at a reviewed release). Every OTHER shape renders true, including
       absent, a JSON null, a string and a number, none of which enables
       anything on its own. Establishing the KIND before using the value is what
       keeps that safe: `eq X false` ERRORS whenever X is not a bool, measured
       2026-08-02, and a template error loses the whole file.

       Collapsing the array form to `true` would keep the plugin on and drop its
       pin, returning it to unconstrained. That fails in the direction that runs
       MORE code (ponytail ships JavaScript lifecycle hooks), so it is not the
       safe default it looks like. No CLI path is known to write the array form,
       so this branch may be unreachable in practice; it is written this way
       because the cost of being wrong is asymmetric.

       WHAT THIS GIVES UP, and it is more than the disable itself. Before this,
       the whole-value write forced every declared plugin back to `true` on
       every apply, so it incidentally RE-ASSERTED plugin state and remediated
       tampering. It no longer does. A live `false` is carried forward for as
       long as it sits in the file, and because the render reproduces it byte
       for byte, `chezmoi status` and `chezmoi diff` print NOTHING for it
       (measured 2026-08-02), which includes `just d`. So any process running as
       this user, which is every agent with Bash under
       `permissions.defaultMode = bypassPermissions`, permanently disables
       `security-guidance` or `superpowers` by writing one boolean, and nothing
       in this repo detects it or restores it. Re-enabling is a deliberate act
       too: `claude plugin enable <id>`, per plugin, and `claude plugin disable
       --all` has no `enable --all` counterpart, so recovering from a mass
       disable is one command per declared plugin. The trade goes this way
       because a containment verb a scheduled apply can silently revoke is not
       containment. It is a real cost, not a formality.

       The disable that sticks is against an APPLY, which is not the same as
       "the plugin cannot load". Settings precedence runs user < project <
       local < flag < policy, so a repo whose project settings enable the plugin
       loads it there regardless of this file; Claude Code has a dedicated
       `ineffective-disable` diagnostic for exactly that (both the diagnostic id
       and all five source names are in the shipped 2.1.220 binary, read
       2026-08-02).

       Seven of these come from marketplaces Anthropic does not ship: ponytail,
       worktrunk, last30days, codex, plannotator, pns and strategy. An entry here
       only records that the plugin should be ENABLED once installed, and enabling
       a plugin that is not installed is inert, so the declaration is safe on a
       fresh machine. The marketplaces themselves are declared in the block below.

       `pns@pns` and `strategy@strategy` ARE THIS REPO'S OWN, out of marketplaces
       deployed under ~/.claude/pns-marketplace and ~/.claude/strategy-marketplace
       rather than fetched from anywhere. Installing one is still one deliberate
       command: `claude plugin install pns@pns` fails on 2.1.257 with "Invalid
       channel" (the release-channel parser takes the `@marketplace` suffix as a
       channel name), even though `--help` still documents that
       `plugin@marketplace` spelling. The WORKAROUND on 2.1.257 is the bare
       `claude plugin install pns`, which is what worked on this machine on
       2026-09-01. --- */ -}}
{{- $declaredPlugins := list
    "superpowers@claude-plugins-official"
    "document-skills@anthropic-agent-skills"
    "frontend-design@claude-plugins-official"
    "playwright@claude-plugins-official"
    "security-guidance@claude-plugins-official"
    "swift-lsp@claude-plugins-official"
    "rust-analyzer-lsp@claude-plugins-official"
    "codex@openai-codex"
    "ponytail@ponytail"
    "skill-creator@claude-plugins-official"
    "worktrunk@worktrunk"
    "last30days@last30days-skill"
    "plannotator@plannotator" -}}
{{- /* DARWIN ONLY, so these declarations and .chezmoiignore agree: both the pns
       and the strategy marketplaces are DIRECTORIES on disk, and neither
       directory is deployed on Linux. Declaring a marketplace whose path is not
       there is a refresh that fails at every startup, which the five github ones
       above cannot do. */ -}}
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
{{-   $declaredPlugins = append $declaredPlugins "pns@pns" -}}
{{-   $declaredPlugins = append $declaredPlugins "strategy@strategy" -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- /* No hasKey test: $livePlugins is a map by the guard above, so `index` on
       an absent key returns an untyped nil, whose kind is neither `bool` nor
       `slice`, so the value test below already covers absence. Measured
       2026-08-02 by replacing the hasKey call with a literal `true`: no case in
       test/unit/claude-enabled-plugins.sh changed verdict, which is what makes
       it dead rather than defensive. */ -}}
{{- $enabledPlugins := dict -}}
{{- range $pluginName := $declaredPlugins -}}
{{-   $pluginEnabled := true -}}
{{-   $liveValue := index $livePlugins $pluginName -}}
{{-   $liveValueKind := kindOf $liveValue -}}
{{-   if or (eq $liveValueKind "bool") (eq $liveValueKind "slice") -}}
{{-     $pluginEnabled = $liveValue -}}
{{-   end -}}
{{-   $enabledPlugins = set $enabledPlugins $pluginName $pluginEnabled -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "enabledPlugins" $enabledPlugins $merged -}}

{{- /* --- stable: the non-Anthropic marketplaces, each one AUTO-UPDATING.

       Claude Code updates a marketplace and its installed plugins at startup on
       its own, so nothing in this repo has to fetch or install anything. What it
       will not do unasked is update a marketplace Anthropic does not publish.
       Read out of the shipped 2.1.220 binary on 2026-08-03: an entry with no
       `autoUpdate` falls back to membership of a built-in set of Anthropic
       marketplace names (claude-plugins-official, anthropic-agent-skills and
       siblings), so every marketplace declared here defaults to NOT updating and
       its plugins sit at whatever version they were installed at, forever, with
       nothing saying so. The schema is
       `autoUpdate: boolean().optional().describe("Whether to automatically
       update this marketplace and its installed plugins on startup")`.

       WHOLE ENTRY PER MARKETPLACE, not a lone autoUpdate flag, because the same
       schema declares `source` REQUIRED, so writing the flag alone onto a fresh
       machine would produce an entry Claude Code rejects. The repo owns these
       seven keys and nothing else under extraKnownMarketplaces: setValueAtPath
       writes at the path, so a marketplace the operator adds through
       `claude plugin marketplace add` keeps its own entry untouched (measured
       2026-08-03 on chezmoi 2.62.3: a sibling key survives, and a parent that
       arrives as a JSON null, an array or a string is replaced by a map rather
       than erroring, so no kind guard is needed here).

       `installLocation` is deliberately not declared. The schema calls it
       auto-generated when absent, and it holds an absolute cache path that
       differs per machine. --- */ -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "extraKnownMarketplaces.ponytail" (dict
    "source" (dict "source" "github" "repo" "DietrichGebert/ponytail")
    "autoUpdate" true) $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "extraKnownMarketplaces.openai-codex" (dict
    "source" (dict "source" "github" "repo" "openai/codex-plugin-cc")
    "autoUpdate" true) $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "extraKnownMarketplaces.worktrunk" (dict
    "source" (dict "source" "github" "repo" "max-sixty/worktrunk")
    "autoUpdate" true) $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "extraKnownMarketplaces.last30days-skill" (dict
    "source" (dict "source" "github" "repo" "mvanhorn/last30days-skill")
    "autoUpdate" true) $merged -}}
{{- $merged = setValueAtPath "extraKnownMarketplaces.plannotator" (dict
    "source" (dict "source" "github" "repo" "backnotprop/plannotator")
    "autoUpdate" true) $merged -}}
{{- /* THIS REPO'S OWN MARKETPLACE, a DIRECTORY rather than a repo to fetch.
       `{source: "directory", path: <absolute>}` is the shape `claude plugin
       marketplace add <path>` writes into user settings, read back off a real
       registration on 2.1.251. NO `autoUpdate`: chezmoi is what updates this
       one, so asking Claude Code to refresh it at startup would be a second
       updater for a directory that is already converged on every apply. */ -}}
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
{{-   $merged = setValueAtPath "extraKnownMarketplaces.pns" (dict
      "source" (dict "source" "directory" "path" (printf "%s/.claude/pns-marketplace" $home))) $merged -}}
{{-   $merged = setValueAtPath "extraKnownMarketplaces.strategy" (dict
      "source" (dict "source" "directory" "path" (printf "%s/.claude/strategy-marketplace" $home))) $merged -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- /* --- stable: skill tiering (on-demand skills load only when the user
